title: Which number is bigger?
mode: draw
chapter: Arithmetic
check: behaves
parts: and or not chip:my_xor
needs: my_xor
---- intro
Is `a1 a0` bigger than `b1 b0`? Compare numbers the way you do on paper, from the **left**:

- if `a1` is 1 and `b1` is 0, `a` is bigger, whatever the right bits are
- if the left bits are the **same**, the right bits decide: `a0` is 1 and `b0` is 0

So `gt` is (`a1` AND NOT `b1`) OR (the left bits are the same AND `a0` AND NOT `b0`). "The same" is your XOR, turned round.
---- task
Light `gt` when the number `a1 a0` is bigger than `b1 b0`.
---- start
input a1 0 0
input b1 0 70
input a0 0 180
input b0 0 250
led gt 640 120
---- reference
input a1, b1, a0, b0
led gt
gt = (a1 & ~b1) | (~(a1 ^ b1) & a0 & ~b0)
---- answer
input a1 0 0
input b1 0 70
input a0 0 180
input b0 0 250
not nb1 120 70
and g1 240 20
chip x1 my_xor 120 120
not nx 240 120
not nb0 120 250
and g2 240 200
and g3 380 160
or o1 500 120
led gt 640 120
b1 -> nb1.in
a1 -> g1.0
nb1 -> g1.1
a1 -> x1.a
b1 -> x1.b
x1.y -> nx.in
b0 -> nb0.in
a0 -> g2.0
nb0 -> g2.1
nx -> g3.0
g2 -> g3.1
g1 -> o1.0
g3 -> o1.1
o1 -> gt
---- hint1
Build the two cases separately: "left bit wins" is `a1` AND NOT `b1`; "right bit wins" is `a0` AND NOT `b0`.
---- hint2
The right bit only counts when the left bits are the same: AND it with NOT (my_xor of `a1` and `b1`). Then OR the two cases.
---- solution
`a1` AND NOT `b1` catches a bigger left bit. my_xor of the left bits, turned round, says they are equal; ANDed with `a0` AND NOT `b0`, it catches a bigger right bit. An OR joins the two cases.
---- done
That was a hard one: seven gates and a chip, each a piece of a sentence. Comparing from the most important bit down is how every processor does it.
